### ✅ How the Model Learned (OLS in Plain Terms)

The model used **Ordinary Least Squares (OLS)** — a math formula that finds the best straight-line fit between all 13 features and the house price (`MEDV`). Where this <model = LinearRegression().fit(df.drop(columns=['MEDV']), df['MEDV'])> becomes this <$12.9k**?> as you'll see below.

It solved this equation once:

> **β = (XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy**

Where:
- X = all 506 rows of features (like `CRIM`, `RM`, `TAX`, etc.)
- y = the actual house prices (`MEDV`)
- β = the **weights (coefficients)** — one per feature

These weights are **stored in memory** inside the `model` object — never saved to disk, never updated after training.

